Given vector field, \[\vec{F}=a_xy-a_yx\] The line integral is \[\int_C \vec{F}\cdot d\vec{l}\] Along the parabola, \[y=x^2\] From the figure, the path is from \((-1,1)\) to \((2,4)\). Now, \[\vec{F}\cdot d\vec{l}
=
y\,dx-x\,dy\] Using \[y=x^2,
\qquad
dy=2x\,dx\] we get \[\vec{F}\cdot d\vec{l}
=
x^2dx-x(2x\,dx)\] \[\vec{F}\cdot d\vec{l}
=
-x^2dx\] Hence, \[\int_C \vec{F}\cdot d\vec{l}
=
\int_{-1}^{2} (-x^2)\,dx\] \[=
-\left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_{-1}^{2}\] \[=
-\left(\frac{8}{3}+\frac{1}{3}\right)\] \[=-3\] \[\boxed{-3.00}\]

Given vector field, \[\vec{F}
=
a_x(5y-k_1z)
+
a_y(3z+k_2x)
+
a_z(k_3y-4x)\] For a conservative field, \[\nabla \times \vec{F}=0\] Using curl condition, \[\frac{\partial F_z}{\partial y}
=
\frac{\partial F_y}{\partial z}\] \[k_3=3\] Also, \[\frac{\partial F_x}{\partial z}
=
\frac{\partial F_z}{\partial x}\] \[-k_1=-4\] \[k_1=4\] Further, \[\frac{\partial F_y}{\partial x}
=
\frac{\partial F_x}{\partial y}\] \[k_2=5\] Hence, \[k_1=4,\qquad k_2=5,\qquad k_3=3\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) $k_1=4,\;k_2=5,\;k_3=3$}}\]

