For a conservative field, \[\nabla \times \vec{F}=0\] Given, \[\vec{F}
=
\hat{a}_x(5y-k_1z)
+
\hat{a}_y(3z+k_2x)
+
\hat{a}_z(k_3y-4x)\] Let, \[F_x=5y-k_1z,\qquad
F_y=3z+k_2x,\qquad
F_z=k_3y-4x\] Now, \[\nabla \times \vec{F}
=
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{a}_x &amp; \hat{a}_y &amp; \hat{a}_z\\
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x} &amp; \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y} &amp; \dfrac{\partial}{\partial z}\\
F_x &amp; F_y &amp; F_z
\end{vmatrix}\] For \(\hat{a}_x\) component, \[\frac{\partial F_z}{\partial y}
-
\frac{\partial F_y}{\partial z}
=
k_3-3=0\] \[k_3=3\] For \(\hat{a}_y\) component, \[\frac{\partial F_x}{\partial z}
-
\frac{\partial F_z}{\partial x}
=
(-k_1)-(-4)=0\] \[k_1=4\] For \(\hat{a}_z\) component, \[\frac{\partial F_y}{\partial x}
-
\frac{\partial F_x}{\partial y}
=
k_2-5=0\] \[k_2=5\] Therefore, \[k_1=4,\qquad k_2=5,\qquad k_3=3\] \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Conservative field condition: \[\nabla\times\vec{F}=0\]</li><li>Each component of curl must be individually equal to zero.</li></ul></div>

Given: <ul><li>Number of turns: \[N_{A}=20,\qquad
N_{B}=20,\qquad
N_{C}=20,\qquad
N_{X}=2\]</li><li>Applied balanced three-phase voltages: \[\bar{V}_{A}=230\angle 0^\circ\,\text{V}\] \[\bar{V}_{B}=230\angle (-120^\circ)\,\text{V}\] \[\bar{V}_{C}=230\angle 120^\circ\,\text{V}\]</li></ul> Assuming winding polarity from the figure: \[\bar{V}_{\text{net}}
=
\bar{V}_{A}
-
\bar{V}_{B}
-
\bar{V}_{C}\] First calculate: \[\bar{V}_{B}+\bar{V}_{C}
=
230\angle (-120^\circ)
+
230\angle 120^\circ\] \[\bar{V}_{B}+\bar{V}_{C}
=
230\left[
(-0.5-j0.866)
+
(-0.5+j0.866)
\right]\] \[\bar{V}_{B}+\bar{V}_{C}
=
-230\angle 0^\circ\,\text{V}\] Hence, \[\bar{V}_{\text{net}}
=
230\angle 0^\circ
-
\left(-230\angle 0^\circ\right)\] \[\bar{V}_{\text{net}}
=
460\angle 0^\circ\,\text{V}\] Using transformer turns ratio: \[\frac{N_{A}}{N_{X}}
=
\frac{20}{2}
=
10\] Therefore, \[V_{X}
=
\frac{460}{10}
=
46\,\text{V}\] <u>Technical flaw:</u> Windings \(A\), \(B\), and \(C\) are placed on the same magnetic limb and therefore share the same mutual flux: \[\phi(t)\] For ideal coupled windings on the same magnetic path, induced EMFs must either be in phase or exactly \(180^\circ\) apart. Applying three independent sources displaced by \(120^\circ\) across windings on the same magnetic limb violates magnetic consistency and Kirchhoff's Voltage Law. Hence, the configuration becomes physically inconsistent and the question was officially awarded Marks To All (MTA). \[\boxed{\text{MTA}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Windings on the same magnetic limb share identical mutual flux.</li><li>Transformer induced voltages depend on common magnetic flux linkage.</li><li>Three independent phase-shifted sources cannot simultaneously impose incompatible flux conditions.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not directly superimpose voltages without checking magnetic feasibility.</li><li>Do not assume ideal transformer constraints can be violated.</li><li>Always verify whether winding placement causes shared flux coupling.</li></ul></div>

Given: No load current is 5,A \[V = 250\,\text{V}, \quad R_{a} = 0.2\,\Omega, \quad R_{sh} = 100\,\Omega\] \[I_{L} = 50\,\text{A}, \quad N_{1} = 1200\,\text{rpm}\] Brush resistance drop: \[1\,\text{V per brush} \Rightarrow \text{Total B.R.D} = 2\,\text{V}\] Shunt field current, \[I_{sh} = \frac{250}{100} = 2.5\,\text{A}\] At no-load, \[I_{a0} = 2.5\,\text{A}\] At loaded condition, \[I_{aL} = I_{L} - I_{sh}\] \[I_{aL} = 50 - 2.5 = 47.5\,\text{A}\] Back emf at no-load, \[E_{b1} = V - I_{a0}R_{a} - \text{B.R.D}\] \[E_{b1} = 250 - 2.5(0.2) - 2\] \[E_{b1} = 247.5\,\text{V}\] Back emf at load, \[E_{b2} = 250 - 47.5(0.2) - 2\] \[E_{b2} = 238.5\,\text{V}\] Using speed relation, \[\frac{N_{2}}{N_{1}} = \frac{E_{b2}}{E_{b1}} \times \frac{\phi_{1}}{\phi_{2}}\] Given, \[\phi_{2} = 0.95\phi_{1}\] \[\frac{N_{2}}{1200}
=
\frac{238.5}{247.5}\times\frac{\phi_{1}}{0.95\phi_{1}}\] \[N_{2} = 1217.22\,\text{rpm}\] \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For DC motors, speed is proportional to \(\dfrac{E_{b}}{\phi}\).</li><li>Shunt field current remains nearly constant as it is connected across supply voltage.</li><li>Brush drop must be included while calculating back emf.</li></ul></div>

Synchronous speed is \[N_s=\frac{120f}{P}
=\frac{120\times 50}{4}
=1500\,\text{rpm}\] No-load speed: \[N_{nl}=N_s(1-s_{nl})
=1500(1-0.01)
=1485\,\text{rpm}\] Full-load speed: \[N_{fl}=N_s(1-s_{fl})
=1500(1-0.05)
=1425\,\text{rpm}\] Percentage speed regulation is \[\%\,\text{regulation}
=
\frac{N_{nl}-N_{fl}}{N_{fl}}\times 100\] \[\%\,\text{regulation}
=
\frac{1485-1425}{1425}\times 100
=4.21\] \[\boxed{4.21}\]

Let the transformed voltages be \[V_a' = t_iV_i\] \[V_b' = t_jV_j\] <img src="img/3e68a85825873c49.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,352px)"> Current flowing through the admittance \(Y\) is \[\frac{I_i}{t_i}
=
(V_a'-V_b')Y\] Substituting transformed voltages, \[\frac{I_i}{t_i}
=
(t_iV_i-t_jV_j)Y\] Therefore, \[I_i
=
t_i^2YV_i
-
t_it_jYV_j\] Similarly, \[\frac{I_j}{t_j}
=
(V_b'-V_a')Y\] \[\frac{I_j}{t_j}
=
(t_jV_j-t_iV_i)Y\] Hence, \[I_j
=
-t_it_jYV_i
+
t_j^2YV_j\] These equations can be written in matrix form as \[\begin{bmatrix}
I_i\\
I_j
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
t_i^2Y &amp; -t_it_jY\\
-t_it_jY &amp; t_j^2Y
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_i\\
V_j
\end{bmatrix}\] Therefore, \[Y_{\text{Bus}}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
t_i^2Y &amp; -t_it_jY\\
-t_it_jY &amp; t_j^2Y
\end{bmatrix}\] \[\boxed{\text{(C)} \enspace
\begin{bmatrix}
t_i^2Y &amp; -t_it_jY\\
-t_it_jY &amp; t_j^2Y
\end{bmatrix}}\]

Given: \[V_1=1.1\,\text{pu}\] \[V_2=1\,\text{pu}\] For a lossless transmission line, reactive power sent from bus 1 is \[Q_{12}
=
\frac{V_1V_2}{X}\cos(\delta_1-\delta_2)
-
\frac{V_2^2}{X}\] Since real power flow is zero, \[\delta_1=\delta_2\] Therefore, \[\cos(\delta_1-\delta_2)=1\] Hence, \[Q_{12}
=
\frac{V_1V_2}{X}
-
\frac{V_2^2}{X}\] Substituting values, \[Q_{12}
=
\frac{1.1\times1}{X}
-
\frac{1^2}{X}\] \[Q_{12}
=
\frac{0.1}{X}\] Reactive power is increased by \(20\%\). \[Q_{12}'=1.2Q_{12}\] \[Q_{12}'
=
\frac{0.12}{X}\] Let the new voltage magnitude at bus 1 be \(V_1'\). Again using the reactive power expression, \[\frac{V_1'V_2}{X}
-
\frac{V_2^2}{X}
=
\frac{0.12}{X}\] Substituting \(V_2=1\) pu, \[\frac{V_1'}{X}
-
\frac{1}{X}
=
\frac{0.12}{X}\] \[V_1'-1=0.12\] \[V_1'=1.12\,\text{pu}\] Therefore, \[\boxed{1.12}\]

The diode current equation is, taking \(\eta=1\), \[I=I_0 e^{V_D/V_T}\] We need the percentage uncertainty in current due to uncertainties in \(V_T\) and \(V_D\). Step 1: Take logarithm Taking natural logarithm, \[\ln I=\ln I_0+\frac{V_D}{V_T}\] Step 2: Sensitivity with respect to \(V_T\) Differentiating w.r.t \(V_T\), \[\frac{1}{I}\frac{\partial I}{\partial V_T}
=
-\frac{V_D}{V_T^2}\] Thus, \[\left|\frac{\partial I}{\partial V_T}\right|
=
\frac{V_D}{V_T^2}I\] Step 3: Sensitivity with respect to \(V_D\) Differentiating w.r.t \(V_D\), \[\frac{1}{I}\frac{\partial I}{\partial V_D}
=
\frac{1}{V_T}\] Hence, \[\left|\frac{\partial I}{\partial V_D}\right|
=
\frac{I}{V_T}\] Step 4: Use uncertainty propagation formula For independent uncertainties, \[W_I=
\sqrt{
\left(
\frac{\partial I}{\partial V_T}W_{V_T}
\right)^2
+
\left(
\frac{\partial I}{\partial V_D}W_{V_D}
\right)^2
}\] Dividing by \(I\), \[\frac{W_I}{I}
=
\sqrt{
\left(
\frac{V_D}{V_T^2}W_{V_T}
\right)^2
+
\left(
\frac{1}{V_T}W_{V_D}
\right)^2
}\] Step 5: Substitute values \[V_T=29\,\text{mV}=0.029\,\text{V}\] \[W_{V_T}=2\,\text{mV}=0.002\,\text{V}\] \[V_D=0.02\,\text{V}\] \[W_{V_D}=1\,\text{mV}=0.001\,\text{V}\] Therefore, \[\frac{W_I}{I}
=
\sqrt{
\left(
\frac{0.02}{(0.029)^2}(0.002)
\right)^2
+
\left(
\frac{0.001}{0.029}
\right)^2
}\] \[\frac{W_I}{I}
=
\sqrt{0.002262+0.001189}\] \[=
\sqrt{0.003451}
=0.0587\] Percentage uncertainty: \[0.0587\times100=5.87\%\] \[\boxed{5.87}\]
